message.content:
I examined the full radiograph and then inspected every root apex at 5–6× zoom (upper and lower quadrants, contrast-enhanced). Most apices show intact lamina dura and normal trabecular bone. Two clearly abnormal periapical/peri-implant radiolucent zones stand out:

1. **Apex of the root-canal-treated upper left first premolar (~tooth 24)** — a well-defined radiolucent halo surrounding the apex of the obturated root (below the sinus floor, distinct from the antral shadow above it). Classic endodontic periapical lesion → **PAI 4**.
2. **Apex of the mesial (left central incisor) implant** — a distinctly darker radiolucent cap wrapping the fixture apex and extending along its distal aspect (asymmetric compared with the contralateral implant, and below the nasal floor line, so not the incisive canal/nasal shadow) → peri-implant radiolucency, graded on the same scale as **PAI 4**.

Coordinates are fractions of image width/height from the top-left corner:

```json
{
  "lesions": [
    {"box": [0.671, 0.336, 0.716, 0.403], "pai": 4},
    {"box": [0.504, 0.310, 0.552, 0.400], "pai": 4}
  ]
}
```

Notes on grading: both were called 4 ("well-defined radiolucent areas") rather than 3 (diffuse mineral-loss changes only) because each shows a circumscribed dark zone centered on the apex; neither shows the aggressive, ill-defined expansile pattern of PAI 5. The lower arch and remaining upper teeth showed no convincing periapical rarefaction at this resolution — the repeated dark wedges between posterior crowns are interproximal/occlusal overlap shadows, not periapical lesions (though several crowns do contain what look like carious defects, which are outside the scope of periapical scoring).
